INT. CHEESE AGING CAVE - NIGHT

Limestone walls sweat. Racks of wheels, floor to ceiling. Cold. Blue work-lights hum.

OSEI ADJEI-BOATENG, 40s, hairnet, rubber apron, taps a wheel with a brass mallet. Listens. Moves on.

PRIYA RAMANATHAN, 20s, clipboard, headlamp, trails him. New hire. Overeager.

PRIYA
Eleven months on that one?

OSEI
Fourteen.

PRIYA
It sounds hollow.

OSEI
It sounds fine.

He keeps walking. She keeps writing.

A rack of Alpine wheels. Humidity gauge. Everything in order.

PRIYA
Nobody else works nights?

OSEI
Nobody else wants to.

PRIYA
Why do you?

OSEI
Quiet.

A drip. Somewhere deep in the cave, metal clicks. Soft. Regular.

Priya glances up. Osei does not.

PRIYA
Is that --

OSEI
Pipes.

Beat. The clicking stops.

Priya relaxes. Laughs at herself.

PRIYA
Sorry. First week. Everything's a ghost.

OSEI
There's coffee in the cold room. Go. Warm up.

PRIYA
You sure?

OSEI
Go.

She goes. Her headlamp swings away down the aisle.

Osei waits. Counts her footsteps. Silence.

He crouches. Pulls a wheel half out of the lowest rack. Behind it: a gap. Duffel bag.

He unzips it. Cash. Banded. Stacks of it.

A RADIO crackles on his belt.

MARGUERITE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Osei. Dock camera shows a van.

OSEI
(low)
Not mine.

MARGUERITE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Nobody's on the schedule tonight. Nobody.

Osei zips the bag. Stands. Looks toward the cold room.

OSEI
(into radio)
The new girl. Who hired her?

MARGUERITE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Nobody hired her.

Osei's hand goes to the mallet.

Footsteps. Not Priya's. Heavy. Behind him.

DMITRI VOLKOV, 50s, wet wool coat, gray stubble, steps out from between two racks. Hands in pockets. Smiling like a man at a funeral reception.

DMITRI
Osei. Good. Saved me the search.

OSEI
You're early.

DMITRI
She's very good, no? The girl. Two weeks of paperwork. Fourteen months of cheese.

Priya returns. No clipboard. No headlamp. A small pistol, held steady.

PRIYA
(flat)
Bag.

Osei lifts the duffel. Slowly.

OSEI
Your coffee's cold.

PRIYA
So are you.

Dmitri takes the bag. Weighs it in one hand.

DMITRI
Count it.

PRIYA
I did. Last Tuesday.

Osei looks at the mallet. At the wheel. At the exit.

MARGUERITE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Osei? Osei, answer me.

Dmitri plucks the radio off his belt. Turns it off.

DMITRI
She talks a lot, your wife.

Osei's jaw sets.

OSEI
She's calling the police.

DMITRI
She is calling the dock. The dock is mine.

Priya cocks the pistol. The click echoes down the cave. The same click from before.

Osei closes his eyes.

OSEI
Pipes.

PRIYA
Pipes.
